# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. `.env.local` is gitignored; this file is not,
# so never put a real token here.

# GitHub personal access token. Optional — without it ingestion runs anonymously
# at 60 requests/hour, which is enough for a guest profile preview but not for
# the ingestion walks: one activity lookup spends up to 3 requests (the feed's
# 300-event ceiling), and one repository lookup up to 10.
#
# With a token the budget is 5000 requests/hour.
#
# A classic token needs no scopes for public read-only data, which is all this
# project uses. Do not grant `repo`, `workflow` or any write scope.
#
# Read only on the server, in `createProfileClient` (lib/github/profile.ts). It
# is never returned to the browser — the API routes return a fixed response
# body that has no field for it. See TASK 4.1's "Secret handling" note.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# GitHub OAuth app (TASK 9.1). Create one at
# https://github.com/settings/developers ("New OAuth App"). The callback URL
# must match GITHUB_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI exactly.
#
# The app requests exactly one scope — `read:user` — which reads the
# authenticated login and nothing more. See lib/auth/oauth.ts.
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GITHUB_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/auth/github/callback

# Signs the session cookie (TASK 9.1). Generate a long random value, e.g.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# Rotating it invalidates every existing session.
SESSION_SECRET=

# Supabase connection string (TASK 10.1). The deployment target is Supabase,
# reached over `pg` — see lib/db/postgres.ts.
#
# In the Supabase dashboard: Project Settings → Database → Connection string →
# "Session pooler" (port 5432). It looks like:
#
#   postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# ## Why `sslmode=no-verify` and not `verify-full`
#
# The pooler's certificate is issued by "Supabase Intermediate 2021 CA" — a
# private CA that is not in Node's bundled trust store. `verify-full` therefore
# fails with "self-signed certificate in certificate chain" against the real
# Supabase server, not against a proxy; this was checked directly (see the
# diagnostic note in docs/DEPLOYMENT.md §1.2).
#
# `no-verify` still encrypts the connection — `pg` maps it to
# `rejectUnauthorized: false` — it just does not authenticate the server, so a
# man-in-the-middle between this process and Supabase could present its own
# certificate. For a database connection whose host is in a variable we control
# and whose traffic is already authenticated with the DB password, that is the
# tradeoff Supabase's pooler imposes; the alternative is adding their CA to the
# Node trust store with NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS.
#
# With this unset the app still runs, on in-memory stores: every account, coin
# balance and purchase is lost on restart. Set it before deploying.
#
# Then apply the schema and the catalogues it needs:
#   npm run db:migrate
DATABASE_URL=

# Structured logging (TASK 21.3). The minimum level emitted: `debug`, `info`,
# `warn` or `error`. Everything the server logs is one JSON line (see
# lib/ops/logger.ts); a field named like a credential, and any value of a secret
# this process holds, is redacted before the line is written. Defaults to `info`;
# set `debug` while diagnosing rate limits or sync counters.
LOG_LEVEL=